        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acp): ACP session startup fails when backend does not advertise thinking config",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes #103802 — Every `sessions_spawn(runtime: \"acp\")` against backends like OpenCode (which only advertise `mode, model` as supported config options) fails immediately with:\n\n```\nAcpRuntimeError: ACP backend \"acpx\" does not accept config key \"thinking\".: code=ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL\n```\n\nThe ACP runtime resolves a thinking default (e.g., `\"off\"` for non-reasoning provider models) and sends it via `setConfigOption`, which the backend rejects. The caller cannot avoid this — passing no `thinking` in `sessions_spawn` does not prevent the injection, and omitting `agents.defaults.thinkingDefault` doesn't either.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nA single prevention layer in `buildRuntimeConfigOptionPairs` (`runtime-options.ts`): a `shouldEmitThinkingConfigOption` guard that checks whether the backend advertises any thinking alias (`thinking`, `effort`, `reasoning_effort`, `thought_level`). If the backend provides an explicit capability list and none of the aliases are present, the thinking pair is never emitted. Follows the exact same pattern as the existing `shouldEmitTimeoutConfigOption` guard.\n\nNo safety net is needed — the prevention layer handles automatic defaults, and explicit user-provided thinking values that a backend doesn't support still fail visibly through the existing strict advertised-keys check in `applyManagerRuntimeControls`. This preserves fail-visible behavior for explicit intent while silently skipping auto-derived defaults.\n\n## User Impact\n\nACP spawns targeting backends with limited config support (mode+model only) succeed instead of failing on init. Backends that support thinking/effort continue to work as before. Explicit `sessions_spawn(thinking: \"high\")` on a non-supporting backend still fails visibly.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2 source files changed, 61 insertions, 1 deletion\n- Follows the existing `shouldEmitTimeoutConfigOption` pattern exactly\n- **7 new tests** in `runtime-options.test.ts`: thinking omitted when unadvertised, emitted for each alias (`thinking`, `effort`, `reasoning_effort`, `thought_level`), emitted when keys unknown, doesn't affect other options\n- All 34 tests pass, `oxlint` reports 0 errors, 0 warnings\n\n**Terminal evidence (commit 1b01f66):**\n\n```\n$ pnpm vitest run src/acp/control-plane/manager.runtime-config.test.ts src/acp/control-plane/runtime-options.test.ts\n ✓ ../../src/acp/control-plane/runtime-options.test.ts (15 tests) 6ms\n ✓ ../../src/acp/control-plane/manager.runtime-config.test.ts (19 tests) 24ms\n Test Files  2 passed (2)\n      Tests  34 passed (34)\n\n$ pnpm exec oxlint src/acp/control-plane/manager.runtime-controls.ts src/acp/control-plane/runtime-options.ts\nFound 0 warnings and 0 errors.\n```\n\n**Live validation:** The same prevention-layer logic was applied as a compiled JS patch to a running OpenClaw instance targeting an OpenCode ACP backend. Before the patch, every ACP spawn failed with `ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL`. After the patch, the thinking config option was silently skipped and session startup completed normally through a full first turn.",
